PiST — third-party components and their licences
================================================

PiST itself is free software under the GNU General Public License, version 2 or
later. See LICENSE.

PiST *drives* the components below as separate programs, passing command-line
arguments. It does not link against them, does not modify them, and does not
write their configuration files. That separation is deliberate and is what keeps
the licensing straightforward (see docs/PLAN.md §7).


Runtime dependencies (invoked as subprocesses)
----------------------------------------------

vasmm68k_mot — the assembler
    Copyright (c) 2002-2026 Volker Barthelmann.
    NOT free software. Its terms permit redistribution *without modification*
    and use for non-commercial purposes; commercial use requires the author's
    written consent except for the M68k/AmigaOS case.
    Source and licence: http://sun.hasenbraten.de/vasm/

    Redistribution: release archives of PiST *do* include a vasm binary, compiled
    by the release workflow from the author's unmodified source tarball, pinned by
    checksum. The basis for this is the permission above: redistribution without
    modification, for non-commercial use. PiST is free software under the GPL and
    is not sold. Note that no binaries are published by the author for host
    platforms at all — only Amiga-family and Atari builds exist — so compiling it
    is the only way to ship a working assembler, and shipping one is the
    difference between an IDE that runs on first launch and one that does not.

    Two commitments follow from that basis, and both are enforced by tooling
    rather than convention:
      - the binary is NEVER patched or modified. PiST drives vasm purely through
        command-line arguments.
      - it is NEVER committed to the PiST source repository, which stays entirely
        free software. It is produced at release time only.

    If you are building PiST for commercial distribution, the permission above
    does not extend to you: supply your own assembler, or obtain the author's
    consent. PiST supports an explicit assembler path in Project Settings for
    exactly this reason.

hatari — the emulator
    GNU General Public License, version 2 or later. Hatari's own readme states
    that linking it statically or dynamically makes a combined work, which is one
    reason PiST runs it as a separate process.
    https://www.hatari-emu.org/

A TOS ROM image is also required. Original Atari TOS images remain copyrighted
and are NEVER distributed with PiST; the user supplies one. The ROM's version is
read from the image header, and TOS 1.04 or later is required for the
GEMDOS-hard-disk run path.

EmuTOS is bundled instead, so that a fresh install runs:
    EmuTOS 1.4, the 1024k emulator image (etos1024k.img),
    GNU General Public License version 2.
    One image covers ST through Falcon and is the same build Hatari ships.
    Verified to autostart a program from the GEMDOS hard disk, which is what the
    Run and debug path requires.
    Source: https://emutos.sourceforge.net/


